<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why StretchesFor puts safety, privacy, and clear movement guidance ahead of engagement."><article class="container-shell py-14 sm:py-20"><div class="max-w-3xl"><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand">About StretchesFor</p><h1 class="mt-3 text-balance text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.05em] sm:text-6xl">Movement guidance with responsible limits.</h1><p class="mt-5 text-pretty text-lg leading-8 text-muted">StretchesFor helps people organise what they feel, recognise when exercise is not the right next step, and follow gentle movement with better instructions.</p></div><div class="prose-sf mt-14 max-w-3xl"><h2>What this tool does</h2><p>It starts with one body area, presents several possible educational patterns, asks a short deterministic questionnaire, and returns one of three paths: gentle movement, professional evaluation, or urgent care guidance.</p><h2>What it does not do</h2><p>It does not diagnose conditions, estimate probabilities, replace a physical examination, or create a medical record. There is no generative AI diagnosis and no attempt to keep someone exercising when their answers suggest that care is more appropriate.</p><h2>How decisions are made</h2><p>Every outcome follows visible, deterministic rules stored with the site. Urgent warning signs take precedence over all other answers. Critical uncertainty is treated conservatively rather than being counted as a “no.”</p><h2>Content approach</h2><p>Pattern and safety copy is based on cited public guidance from recognised health systems and medical organisations. The content is educational and has not been individually medically reviewed unless a page explicitly says otherwise.</p></div></article></BaseLayout>
